WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
She would always grab my hand after we finished the check-up.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:11.005
Her warm fingers, the exact shape of which I can only guess at to this day, pressed against mine with an urgent yet methodical tenderness, searching.

00:00:11.305 --> 00:00:19.765
I could see the skin at the corner of my palm sink gently where her thumb should have been, the flesh beneath its translucent outline gradually yielding as it moved toward my index finger.

00:00:20.065 --> 00:00:26.860
After a while, her weary voice would softly describe the X-ray image she had obtained, her diagnosis each time revealing something far beyond my bones.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.570
It was as if she could see right through me, which, in our case, filled me with additional unease.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:40.735
After all, it was I who was staring at a seemingly empty hospital bed, its covers creased beneath the weight of the body occupying it, while I held out my hand to be read by this very same absence.

00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:47.380
I could get lost in her vivid storytelling so easily and imagine her predictions so clearly that I often forgot I could not actually see her.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.045
I know it sounds strange, but despite tending to her body every day, she was, somehow, invisible.

00:00:52.345 --> 00:00:59.140
My eyes could not make out any visual image of her face or form, yet I can still recall the physiological existence that seemed to negate that absence.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.440
I remember how, every morning, when I entered the patient’s room, she was already awake.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:08.600
Soft, sigh-like murmurs hovered above the hollow her body had made in the stack of pillows, giving her away.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:17.540
I remember how, every morning, I was surprised by her voice coming from slightly lower than expected; I thought it was because she had shrunk a little with age, though I couldn’t say for sure.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:29.495
I remember how, every morning, I watched the disc-shaped resonator of my stethoscope leave my side, seemingly floating through the air as if on its own, until it came to rest in the right place against her skin, her steady pulse suddenly amplified in my ears.

00:01:29.795 --> 00:01:33.845
Sometimes the familiar lub-dub echoed so loudly that I couldn’t tell it apart from my own.

00:01:34.145 --> 00:01:42.785
I remember how, every morning, I felt her wrinkled fingers warm mine for a second as they took the thermometer from my hand, before it paused at some indeterminate point, suspended in the air.

00:01:43.085 --> 00:01:49.655
I watched the column of mercury rise slowly as it responded to the warmth of the skin I couldn’t see, gradually revealing whether she had a fever.

00:01:49.955 --> 00:01:57.920
And I remember how, one morning, a Saturday, I believe, not long after all of our every-mornings, the hospital director’s »But, sir, this bill was clearly a mistake on our part!

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:05.510
Your grandmother was never here. She was erased; she never existed« resonated through the room I clearly saw her occupy. It was as if she had completely vanished.
